#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179452 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "db8e7d68-5731-5a44-b86e-919a13689540" :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Upper Riemann Sum for a Strictly Increasing Function"}] :question/id #uuid "b1f5f31f-55d0-5523-8225-03db04d35cb1" :question/math-academy-id "e-6248" :question/problem "Let $R = [0,2]\\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y) = x+2y$. Further, let $P$ be a partition of $R$, where\n\n$P_{1} = {x_{0}, x_{1}, …, x_{m}}, P_{2} = {y_{0}, y_{1}, …, y_{n}}, P = P_{1} \\cdot P_{2}$.\n\nWrite down the upper Riemann sum $U(f, P)$." :question/worked-solution "The upper Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nU(f, P) &= ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)M_{ij}Δx_{i}Δy_{j}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $M_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}$.\n\nWe consider an arbitrary subregion $R_{ij}$ of the partition. This subregion lies between $x_{i-1}$ and $x_i$ over the $x$-axis and $y_{i-1}$ and $y_i$ over the $y$-axis, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3696/Source/Images/edad92d80f1137da0db424cf1d10358c.png)\n\nNotice that\n\n- $f(x,y) = x+2y$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n- $f(x,y) = x+2y$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3696/Source/Images/963f3198c226e0fcf8688c291d514773.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y$. This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3696/Source/Images/6870b71405e9ec1c7bd8f7feb11f4f90.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM_{ij} &= f(x_{i}, y_{j}) = x_{i} + 2y_{j}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all\n\n$$\n1\\leq i\\leq m\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n1\\leq j\\leq n\n$$\n\nFinally, the upper Riemann sum is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nU(f, P) &= ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)(x_{i} + 2y_{j})Δx_{i}Δy_{j}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179453 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5c07033-ef84-52e6-bd08-b1f0cc289e9f" :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Upper Riemann Sum for a Strictly Decreasing Function"}] :question/id #uuid "7215d75e-c001-5c28-a5e7-08a74f6543d7" :question/math-academy-id "e-6250" :question/problem "Let $R = [0,1] \\times [0,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y) = 90-x^2-y^2$.\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R$, where\n\n$P_{1} = {x_{0}, x_{1}, …, x_{m}}, P_{2} = {y_{0}, y_{1}, …, y_{n}}, P = P_{1} \\cdot P_{2}$.\n\nWrite down the upper Riemann sum $U(f, P)$." :question/worked-solution "The upper Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nU(f, P) &= ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)M_{ij}Δx_{i}Δy_{j}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $M_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}$.\n\nWe consider an arbitrary subregion $R_{ij}$ of the partition. This subregion lies between $x_{i-1}$ and $x_i$ over the $x$-axis and $y_{i-1}$ and $y_i$ over the $y$-axis, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3696/Source/Images/f0dc3e5ada5e449af8a26059d5ef0f8c.png)\n\nNotice that\n\n- $f(x,y) = 90-x^2-y^2$ decreases as $x$ increases on $[0,1]$, and\n- $f(x,y) = 90-x^2-y^2$ decreases as $y$ increases on $[0,2]$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3696/Source/Images/3bc69da4098b8e380e39e0546cdce312.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y$. This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3696/Source/Images/fde88ca2195c884141658dc35168ef03.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM_{ij} &= f(x_{i - 1}, y_{j - 1}) = 90 - x_{i - 1}^{2} - y_{j - 1}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all\n\n$$\n1\\leq i\\leq m\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n1\\leq j\\leq n\n$$\n\nFinally, the upper Riemann sum is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nU(f, P) &= ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)(90 - x_{i - 1}^{2} - y_{j - 1}^{2})Δx_{i}Δy_{j}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179519 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b815d42d-8e8a-5036-b6c4-76e4c20b20b7" :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Upper Riemann Sum for a Function That’s Neither Strictly Increasing nor Decreasing"}] :question/id #uuid "79b4e8e3-5cfd-5b28-ba19-f69d2c9e0c71" :question/math-academy-id "e-9495" :question/problem "Let $R = [2,4] \\times [0,1]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y) = 2x - y$. Further, let $P$ be a partition of $R$, where\n$P_{1} = {x_{0}, x_{1}, …, x_{m}}, P_{2} = {y_{0}, y_{1}, …, y_{n}}, P = P_{1} \\cdot P_{2}$.\n\nWrite down the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P)$." :question/worked-solution "The upper Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nU(f, P) &= ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)M_{ij}Δx_{i}Δy_{j}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $M_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}$.\n\nWe consider an arbitrary subregion $R_{ij}$ of the partition. This subregion lies between $x_{i-1}$ and $x_i$ over the $x$-axis and $y_{i-1}$ and $y_i$ over the $y$-axis, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3696/Source/Images/a1b215e94fff6e51b4665cd869553c5c.png)\n\nNotice that\n\n- $f(x,y) = 2x - y$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n- $f(x,y) = 2x - y$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3696/Source/Images/78607f0271a92fd1540c9ddf593f0010.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y$. This is the value at the *bottom right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3696/Source/Images/c7d415e021fb7f02f6aa222664e0ab60.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM_{ij} &= f(x_{i}, y_{j - 1}) = 2x_{i} - y_{j - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all\n\n$$\n1\\leq i\\leq m\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n1\\leq j\\leq n\n$$\n\nFinally, the upper Riemann sum is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nU(f, P) &= ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)(2x_{i} - y_{j - 1})Δx_{i}Δy_{j}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180206 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b815d42d-8e8a-5036-b6c4-76e4c20b20b7" :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Upper Riemann Sum for a Function That’s Neither Strictly Increasing nor Decreasing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac7ff5cd-1258-5598-8e0d-576a88b0b32f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x_{i} - 3y_{j - 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179112} {:answer/id #uuid "ec16f68a-15b3-57fd-9e91-1bc994e24bd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x_{i} - 3y_{j}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179113} {:answer/id #uuid "17b225a6-859b-52c0-9d8d-26c63bbf63df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x_{i - 1} + 5y_{j}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179114} {:answer/id #uuid "1dd95062-d1f0-5d0b-9474-d4d7354c5d76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x_{i - 1} + 5y_{j - 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179115} {:answer/id #uuid "2f7c5167-dd2b-54f9-a925-4bc5d01e39f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x_{i - 1} - 3y_{j - 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179116}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac7ff5cd-1258-5598-8e0d-576a88b0b32f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x_{i} - 3y_{j - 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179112} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8297de0-fe4c-58ea-8ed9-818e7c1ce138" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179111}] :question/id #uuid "8a3bcd08-2d96-5714-8501-bedb2f0a1010" :question/math-academy-id "q-75056" :question/problem "Let $R = [15, 20] \\cdot [3, 5]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x, y) = x - 3y$.\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R$, where\n\n$P_{1} = {x_{0}, x_{1}, …, x_{m}}, P_{2} = {y_{0}, y_{1}, …, y_{n}}, P = P_{1} \\cdot P_{2}$.\n\nGiven that the upper Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to $P$ is given by\n$U(f, P) = ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)AAAAAΔx_{i}Δy_{j}$\n\nwhere $Δx_{i} = x_{i} - x_{i - 1}$ and $Δy_{j} = y_{j} - y_{j - 1}$,\nwhat is the missing expression in the double summation above?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180207 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b815d42d-8e8a-5036-b6c4-76e4c20b20b7" :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Upper Riemann Sum for a Function That’s Neither Strictly Increasing nor Decreasing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc2f39ec-aed9-54b6-802c-d5e9e6f78b9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{j} - 3x_{i - 1} + 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179118} {:answer/id #uuid "c856dcf9-0ef2-55dd-a999-ae2cf061238c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{j - 1} - x_{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179119} {:answer/id #uuid "0ad25943-af7c-5298-a2ee-e164c5ae1123" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{j - 1} - 3x_{i - 1} + 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179120} {:answer/id #uuid "29b34eca-e246-50a1-bea1-4db6a2339d11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{j} - x_{i - 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179121} {:answer/id #uuid "6961846c-3282-5a69-9cb4-34d864aeefc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y_{j} - x_{i - 1} + 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179122}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc2f39ec-aed9-54b6-802c-d5e9e6f78b9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{j} - 3x_{i - 1} + 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179118} :answer-field/id #uuid "65af24b5-79aa-5276-b1f9-ef714e56f848" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179117}] :question/id #uuid "42c305a2-4b3e-513e-a39c-d43ad646b48c" :question/math-academy-id "q-75060" :question/problem "Let $R = [- 3, 2] \\cdot [4, 7]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x, y) = y - 3x + 2$.\nFurther, le